ise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Analysis: River Decision - Check or Bet?**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: We're on the river in a heads-up Texas Hold'em match. I've got a pair of sevens on the board, but my kicker is only a nine. The board is paired (7, 7, A, J, 5), and the pot is small, about 200 chips.  My opponent has been checking consistently throughout the hand, which could mean weakness, but also a trap or a hand that wants to get to showdown.\n\nGiven that my hand is weak and the pot is small, I'm leaning towards checking.  I missed my flush and straight draws, and while a pair of sevens is *something*, it's likely beaten by almost anything the opponent has.  A king-high or queen-high, even a ten-high, beats my nine kicker.  Plus, I could be up against a small pair or even two pair, which I'm obviously losing to.\n\nIf I bet, I might fold out better high cards (K-high, Q-high) which is a small win, but I'm likely getting called by better hands. The pot size doesn't warrant the risk of a bluff with such a weak holding.  The history of checks also makes a bluff unlikely.\n\nChecking allows me to see if I can potentially win a small pot, and it keeps me from losing more chips.  My main goal in this specific hand is to protect my remaining chips at this point in the game.  At this stage, checking is the safest bet.  I'll go with the check and see what the opponent does, hoping for the best but expecting the worst.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: I hold 8d9d on a board of 7s Ad 5d Js 7c. I flopped a flush draw and a gutshot straight draw but missed both by the river. My final hand is a Pair of 7s (from the board) with an Ace, Jack, and 9 kicker (A, J, 7, 7, 9). This is a very weak hand that barely beats playing the board (the board is A, J, 7, 7, 5). Any Ten, Queen, or King in the opponent's hand beats me, as does any pair. The opponent checked the flop and turn, suggesting they don't have a strong hand